```html
<div class="interactive-textbook">

  <div class="section-card">
    <h2>Introduction</h2>
    <p>Newton's Laws of Motion are the foundation of classical mechanics. They describe the relationship between the forces acting on an object and its motion. Understanding these laws is crucial for understanding how objects move and interact in the world around us.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <h2>Newton's First Law (Law of Inertia)</h2>
    <p>An object at rest stays at rest, and an object in motion stays in motion with the same speed and in the same direction unless acted upon by a net external force.</p>
    <p>This is also known as the Law of Inertia. Inertia is the tendency of an object to resist changes in its state of motion.</p>
    <div class="example-card">
      <h3>Example:</h3>
      <p>A book on a table will remain at rest unless someone pushes it. A hockey puck sliding on ice will continue to slide at a constant velocity until friction or the boards stop it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <h2>Newton's Second Law</h2>
    <p>The acceleration of an object is directly proportional to the net force acting on the object, is in the direction of the net force, and is inversely proportional to the object's mass.</p>
    <div class="formula-card">
      <h3>Main Formula</h3>
      <p>
        $$F = ma$$
      </p>
      <button class="formula-button" onclick="explainFormula('F = ma')">Explain Formula</button>
      <button class="formula-button" onclick="openFormulaLab('F = ma')">Open Formula Lab</button>
    </div>
    <div class="example-card">
      <h3>Example:</h3>
      <p>If you push a shopping cart with twice the force, it will accelerate twice as much (assuming the mass stays the same). If you push a heavier shopping cart with the same force, it will accelerate less.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <h2>Newton's Third Law (Law of Action-Reaction)</h2>
    <p>For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction.</p>
    <p>When one object exerts a force on a second object, the second object simultaneously exerts a force equal in magnitude and opposite in direction on the first object.</p>
    <div class="example-card">
      <h3>Example:</h3>
      <p>When you jump, you push down on the ground (action), and the ground pushes back up on you (reaction), propelling you upwards.  A rocket expels exhaust gases downwards (action), and the gases exert an equal and opposite force on the rocket, propelling it upwards (reaction).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <h2>Important Notes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Newton's Laws are most accurate for objects moving at speeds much less than the speed of light.</li>
      <li>These laws are foundational to many areas of physics and engineering.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <h2>Summary</h2>
    <p>Newton's three laws of motion describe the fundamental relationships between forces and motion. The Law of Inertia, the relationship described by F=ma, and the Law of Action-Reaction work together to explain how objects behave in the physical world.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <h2>Suggested Questions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Explain the concept of inertia and provide an example.</li>
      <li>How does Newton's Second Law explain the relationship between force, mass, and acceleration?</li>
      <li>Give an example of Newton's Third Law in action.</li>
      <li>How does friction affect Newton's Laws of Motion?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

</div>

<style>
